  34:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h **** /**
  35:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * @defgroup CO_LSSslave LSS Slave
  36:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * CANopen Layer Setting Service - slave protocol.
  37:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  *
  38:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * @ingroup CO_CANopen_305
  39:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * @{
  40:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * The slave provides the following services
  41:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * - node selection via LSS address
  42:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * - node selection via LSS fastscan
  43:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * - Inquire LSS address of currently selected node
  44:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * - Inquire node ID
  45:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * - Configure bit timing
  46:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * - Configure node ID
  47:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * - Activate bit timing parameters
  48:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * - Store configuration (bit rate and node ID)
  49:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  *
  50:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * After CAN module start, the LSS slave and NMT slave are started and then coexist alongside each 
  51:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * this behaviour, the CANopen node startup process has to be controlled more detailed. Therefore, 
  52:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * invoked between CO_CANinit() and CO_CANopenInit() in the communication reset section.
  53:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  *
  54:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * Moreover, the LSS slave needs to pause the NMT slave initialization in case no valid node ID is 
  55:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * up. In that case CO_CANopenInit() skips initialization of other CANopen modules and CO_process()
  56:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * other modules than LSS slave automatically.
  57:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  *
  58:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * Variables for CAN-bitrate and CANopen node-id must be initialized by application from non-volati
  59:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * switches. Pointers to them are passed to CO_LSSinit() function. Those variables represents pendi
ARM GAS  C:\Users\Lenovo\AppData\Local\Temp\ccLI0PPk.s 			page 59


  60:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * is valid in the moment it enters CO_LSSinit(), it also becomes active node-id and the stack init
  61:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * Otherwise, node-id must be configured by lss and after successful configuration stack passes res
  62:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * autonomously.
  63:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  *
  64:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * Device with all threads can be normally initialized and running despite that node-id is not vali
  65:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * take care, because CANopen is not initialized. In that case CO_CANopenInit() returns error condi
  66:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * CO_ERROR_NODE_ID_UNCONFIGURED_LSS which must be handled properly. Status can also be checked wit
  67:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * CO->nodeIdUnconfigured variable.
  68:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  *
  69:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * Some callback functions may be initialized by application with CO_LSSslave_initCkBitRateCall(),
  70:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  * CO_LSSslave_initActBitRateCall() and CO_LSSslave_initCfgStoreCall().
  71:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h ****  */
  72:CANopen/305/CO_LSSslave.h **** 
